The power set of a 5-element ground set has size \(2^5=32\). Any two complementary sets \(A\) and \(A^c\) are disjoint, and there are exactly 16 complementary pairs. Consequently every intersecting family contains at most one member from each pair and therefore has cardinality at most 16.  

An intersecting family of cardinality exactly 16 must therefore contain precisely one set from every complementary pair. Two families realize this bound:  
- for each fixed element \(i\in\{1,2,3,4,5\}\), the collection of all subsets containing \(i\) (5 such collections);  
- the collection of all subsets of size at least 3 (a single additional collection, which meets the bound because any two sets of size \(\ge3\) intersect when the ground set has odd cardinality 5).  

