To solve this problem, we can treat the indices of the array as nodes in a graph. An edge exists between index $i$ and index $j$ if $\text{gcd}(\text{nums}[i], \text{nums}[j]) > 1$. However, creating edges between all pairs is $O(N^2)$, which is too slow.

Instead, we can use a **Disjoint Set Union (DSU)** to group indices. We can represent each prime factor present in the numbers as a node in our DSU. For every number `nums[i]`, we find all its prime factors and union them together. If all numbers in the array belong to the same connected component in the DSU, then it is possible to traverse between any two indices. We must ignore numbers equal to 1, as they cannot be part of any traversal.

